speechtext:
My answer consisted in reading the statute, and that seems to be perfectly clear. I will read all that relates to this point:
(d)	'county' Includes every town, township, parish and other division or municipality, except a city, within the territorial limits of the county for municipal purposes;
(e)	as respects the province of Ontario, or any other province in which provisional or temporary Judicial districts exist, 'county' includes such provisional or temporary judicial districts;
(f)	as respects the province of Manitoba,
' county ' means the electoral districts therein, as designated by the Representation Act;
(g)	as respects the province of British Columbia, until the province shall have been divided into counties and a regular municipal organization established in each of such counties, 'county' means an electoral district therein in accordance with the division of the province for election of members of the House of Commons of Canada, and includes every town, township, parish and other division or municipality within the territorial limits of such electoral district, or within a union of electoral districts where united for municipal purposes.
It is possible, in view of this first general definition of ' county ' excepting from it the cities, that that first disposition should be considered as applying to these counties as subsequently defined in the other provinces. I shall be glad to look into that matter. I am free to confess that I did not examine that question, because what was represented to us, and the only thing represented to us, was that it was desirable that we should insert a definition so as to make it aplicable to Alberta and Saskatchewan, without any expression of (desire as to how it should be made to apply.
